{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>When a man takes a wife, etc.</b> The Torah speaks against the sons of men, even distinguished men, who turn their faces away from the Torah, as they said: for the day is short for what one needs to exert himself for his livelihood. And concerning them the verse says <b>when a man takes a wife</b> — this is the Torah that is betrothed; <b>and comes to her</b> — for the light of the Torah has already shone upon them at Mount Sinai and they received the tablets; <b>and hates her</b> — meaning, their heart does not desire to give her due conjugal right in judgment. And when an admonisher comes to speak to him: why do you reject the wife of your youth? — he answers that the Torah does not go whoring and provide sustenance, and he needs a livelihood; moreover, he sees no sign of blessing from the exertion of her study — for even if he engages in her, the gates of good livelihood are not opened for him. And on the contrary, he sees that all the people who distanced themselves from her and do not think of her at all — their houses are full of every good. And this is what the verse says: <b>and he frames charges against her and brings an evil name upon her</b>, saying <b>et this woman I took</b> — meaning, he does not deny having taken her, but he says: ״I drew near to her and I did not find in her virginity״ — a strong expression, for her trespassers encamp around to knock on others' doors to find prey." "The verse informs us that the Holy One, blessed be He — who is called the father of the young woman — and Knesset Israel will demand restitution for the insult to the Torah before the Great Court. And this is what it says: <b>and the father of the young woman and her mother shall take and bring out the proof of the young woman's virginity</b> — a strong allusion and ascent, and lofty attainments will be attained through her intercession, and they will demand her insult in court, as it says <b>at the gate</b>. And this is what they said in the Zohar (part 3, 70a): that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to demand restitution for the insult to the Torah, and as they said in the Mishnah (Avot 6:2): woe to the creatures from the insult to the Torah." "<b>And behold</b> — the claim of the one who stands to demand restitution for her insult is written before us, as it says: <b>and the father of the young woman shall say to the elders: my daughter I gave to this man for a wife</b> — meaning, I did not give her to be abandoned in a corner, but to conduct himself with her in the conduct of a wife, that he should not diminish her food, her clothing, and her conjugal right, as the three things are explained in the Zohar (Tikkunim 6) — all of which the Torah needs. <b>And he hated her</b> — here it alludes to the cause of the distancing from her, which comes from that aspect called hatred, and it gives the reason for the distancing: because he found no strength and intensity in her. And this is what it says: <b>and behold, he frames charges</b>, etc. It is precise in saying ״behold, he״ to signal that this is not the truth — that nothing caused it except the hater who dwells within him. <b>And these are the proofs of my daughter's virginity</b>, etc. And the matters will be as clear as the garment — like the many virtues and the intensity of the Torah, for the beginning of every thing, of the entire world, was created through her; for her sake it was created; and through her it is sustained; and every good hidden away for the World to Come, and the treasures of life and good, are acquired through her." "<b>And it says</b> that this shall be the judgment of that man by order of the court. First, <b>they shall discipline him</b>, along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): if a person sees that sufferings come upon him, etc., let him attribute it to neglect of Torah — meaning, on account of what he neglected in the Torah. And the later authorities were pressed to explain this matter with the challenge: but is neglect of a sin not itself a great sin? And why did he not attribute it to it until he found none? And the resolution of the matter is that there is no measure to what is required in the business of Torah, as they said (Avot 2:16): and you are not free to neglect it. And even though it may appear in a person's eyes that he engaged, when he sees sufferings coming upon him without sin, he will know that as it was fitting for him to do, he did not do, and for this he is punished." "And it says <b>and they shall punish him one hundred silver</b> — an allusion to the one hundred blessings one is obligated to recite every day, as they derived (Menahot 43b) from the verse ״ועתה ישראל מה ה׳ אלהיך שואל״ — do not read מה but מאה. And this verse is said concerning a penitent, as they said in the Gemara (Genesis Rabbah 21): ״ועתה״ — ״ועתה״ means nothing other than repentance, as it is written ״ועתה ישראל מה ה׳ אלהיך שואל״, etc. — until here — that one is obligated to exert himself to fulfill them." "And the verse's saying <b>and they shall give to the father of the young woman</b> — all of them are blessings to HaShem, for He is the father of the young woman. And the verse's saying <b>for he put forth an evil name upon the virgin of Israel</b> — this is the Shekhinah, which is the totality of Israel, and she is the tenth sefirah that encompasses one hundred, and she is called Torah she-be-al peh (the Oral Torah), as is well known to those who know grace [ḥen]." "And the verse's saying <b>and she shall be to him for a wife</b>, etc. — meaning: even though there is no obligation in Torah study but only fixed times, a man such as this who put forth an evil name upon her must knock upon her doors all the day and all the night — ״he may not send her away all his days״ (Deuteronomy 22:19). And the like we find for our Rabbis of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 25:1), who said that a penitent, if he was accustomed to study one folio, should study two, and so forth along this path. And this applies to a penitent from other transgressions; but one who puts forth an evil name upon the Torah — his sentence is to sit with her all his days: Torah is his craft." "And the verse's saying <b>״but if the thing was true״</b>, etc. — meaning: if it happened that Torah which this man studied and examined in it had no signs of virginity, that it was Torah of heresy that Saddok and Baytus taught, and such Torah has no permanence and is an evil portion — for such Torah HaShem will command to use it as a stone with which to stone it and to destroy it, as the verse says ״and she shall die.״ Even though these are things that proceeded from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, nevertheless the intention is not so. And our Sages of blessed memory already said (Gittin 45b) concerning a Torah scroll written by a heretic that it must be burned. <b>״because she has wrought a vile thing, to play the harlot״</b>, etc., <b>״so you shall purge the evil״</b> — for this shall be called an evil portion and requires purging."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {4 "The citation 'Genesis Rabbah 21' is imprecise; the identification of ועתה as תשובה/repentance is a well-known midrashic reading found in multiple sources. The phrase עד כאן ('until here') appears to mark the end of a quotation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:56.587762188Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}